{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-135283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/68dc8c639235c6dbc2fe9a238a3855327a0c7da337e5792b7896c30e19b2714e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-135283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\rho =\\frac{\\theta }{4}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "ϕ=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-102949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/e542606b09d564f450aa9c2f4e2de5bf8dc197644a094c37420ee1b881f14e1f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-102949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $2.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-102949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-102949" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-102949" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\rho =2"] ["math" "\\rho =2\\theta "] ["math" "\\rho =2ϕ"] ["math" "\\theta =2"] ["math" "ϕ=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-102949" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\rho =2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/1fa98148cf4302a5524ade655ee09a3f74d248ba766849f62a71f3ec85e860c6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In the picture, we are given a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given plane makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-135246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "ϕ=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "ϕ=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\theta "] ["math" "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/61b3e9163769acbcb5804ccd2a5cbfed9cbbf2f0fc113ff39b64ea7153fbe2f4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $\\sqrt{2}.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-135254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\rho =3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\rho =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\rho =\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\rho =\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/db4700a08fd81ab6d2bc07d80d72fb1fc9edea6c0cdf652b251a755b53a2b148.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $3.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-135247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\rho =3"] ["math" "\\rho =9"] ["math" "\\theta =3\\rho "] ["math" "\\theta =9\\rho "] ["math" "ϕ=3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\rho =3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/3775fdcb48fd015097b484b6d5ebaefcecd4ef10298cb18b34226a8eae1be1ef.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-135300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-line"] ["text" "A sphere"] ["text" "An ellipsoid"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A sphere"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =0$ in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =0\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $0.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/832655d3a5ee1429fb1e1c005faa8c19d2a92ecfcf00cef64cfb165bf4755729.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-135303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane"] ["text" "A half-sphere"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "An ellipsoid"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-plane"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =8$ in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =8\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $8.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/35a2af1537995e83771267fc3ecf28816e9612fc11cead84bb9dbb06ac5e90c5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-135299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capt