\\pi }{6}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/18925bc14927fd75d65ffe1eddbb6f934616e404d3287c95aff1b63b9b499719.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-135296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A line"] ["text" "A plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-cone"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $ϕ=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nϕ=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis must equal $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/ee967c13fa7edaaba4d3a218abf20aa067cbd2251f2a008158c9c991c27344ea.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-135295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/acti