nce/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/6cf95f58e436577be5ad696325ae2d1e7bfd5882b4899e88806c4ae653a5c518.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi /2]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-161824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 12,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /6}"] ["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 36,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}"] ["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}"] ["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /4}"] ["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/dcd901b032075a4c4a22c991d9bb3e5e5ff49e7a2c91e7e840ede1516085f683.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/736c46d0121c646def56c6b8fede81a4155fffb97032facd75a6cdf29f98f65a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-161850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Develop