}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x=0$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{3}{2},0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/7d161ad5adc8f99f81634049c487f299f84edde255b2f08aa3d1dd4060f99e75.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4448bd8007dbda56ee361e201cffd934146376c2c2e57978f6d2f2c2fa1f20d"} {"question" "q-161888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$"] ["text" "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$"] ["text" "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$"] ["text" "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$"] ["text" "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/3e28d562bb7edd9a067bb3ee2c7cae07dc3b76315f39eeed084bdb06682042a2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/70450f3fc9e9971a76e4458c5e020c1f264e739913fbb24d3e6ff22359542316.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b85927b5754fc3d57792c11abf6864fa7d8e65638325052a882f9ab3c40d1a3"} {"question" "q-161844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "